From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OddBlueDog <3959851+OddBlueDog@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 00:03:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: pace a station whose link is poor

The firmware takes turns between the stations it has frames for, a
transmission each, whatever their rates: a station at a low rate then
holds the air most of the time, and one at a high rate beside it carries a
fraction of what it could. A lower airtime weight or AQL limit only gets
the slow one down to a frame a turn.

A station can now have a share of the time (wcid->tx_pace in 1/1000, the
station's debugfs file tx_pace; 0, the default: not paced). While a
station that isn't paced waits for the air (frames left in its queue after
its turn, or a turn that adds to half a millisecond or more of its airtime
still in the hardware), a paced one's best effort and background frames
are held: each frame taken from its queue adds mac80211's estimate of its
airtime to what the station owes, time pays it off at the share, and the
next turn starts when nothing is owed. Voice and video are not held, but
count. Alone or beside light traffic a paced station is sent to as before.

A queue that waits is not returned to mac80211's list, where it would end
every round before the others' turn; an hrtimer puts it back. The file
reads back the share, whether the station is paced now, how many times it
was held and for how long.

KUnit: mt76-tx (the arithmetic, the hold and the timer) and mt76-ps-sim
(a paced station beside a backlogged one on mac80211's stations and
queues).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
